"A quorum intersection diagram explains which replica sets participate in reads and writes and whether their membership overlaps.\n\nUse when: Review read and write acknowledgment requirements. Explain why quorum overlap alone is not a full consistency proof.\n\nAvoid when: Detailed leader-election or message-retry behavior is needed.\n\nVisual grammar: Replica markers form the declared replica universe. Overlays or outlined sets identify read and write participants. Labels state replica count and required acknowledgments without implying probabilities.\n\nBuild: Declare the replication factor and quorum definitions. Select representative read and write replica sets. Show their intersection and relevant failure cases. Keep set borders and replica labels separated, using callouts instead of text on intersections.\n\nCheck: Set membership matches the stated quorum sizes. The intersection condition is evaluated against the same replica universe. Versioning, failed writes, and repair assumptions accompany consistency claims. Overlapping boundaries never obscure replica identities or quorum labels.\n\nPitfalls: Claiming R + W > N alone guarantees linearizability. Counting responses from outside the relevant replica set.\n\nExample: With three replicas, a two-replica write set intersects every two-replica read set, under the stated membership assumptions.\n\nAlternatives: Replication topology, Consensus protocol diagram."
